<p class="gna-lede">Snoring is the sound of soft tissue vibrating in a narrowed airway; obstructive sleep apnea is when that airway repeatedly narrows so much that breathing is reduced or stops, lowering oxygen levels and breaking up sleep. Snoring on its own is usually harmless to health, but snoring combined with pauses in breathing, gasping or daytime sleepiness is a warning sign that deserves a sleep study.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Think of snoring and sleep apnea as points on the same spectrum. At one end is occasional, gentle snoring after a heavy dinner or during a cold. At the other is loud snoring every night with repeated breathing pauses. The key question is not how loudly you snore, but whether your breathing is being interrupted — and whether that is affecting how you feel during the day.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Many adults snore; only a proportion of them have sleep apnea.</li>
    <li>Witnessed pauses, gasping or choking, and unrefreshing sleep point towards sleep apnea.</li>
    <li>Not everyone with sleep apnea snores loudly — this is especially true for women.</li>
    <li>Phone recordings and questionnaires help, but only a sleep study can diagnose sleep apnea.</li>
    <li>Treating snoring without first ruling out sleep apnea can hide a more serious problem.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="why-people-snore">Why people snore</h2>
<p>When you fall asleep, the muscles of the throat relax. If the airway is already a little narrow, air moving through it becomes turbulent and makes the soft palate, uvula and base of the tongue vibrate — that vibration is the snore. Common reasons for a narrow airway include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A blocked nose</strong> — a deviated septum, enlarged turbinates, nasal polyps or allergic rhinitis, which for many people flares with dust, pollen and seasonal changes</li>
  <li><strong>Large tonsils, or a long soft palate and uvula</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Excess weight</strong>, especially around the neck</li>
  <li><strong>Jaw shape</strong> — a small or set-back lower jaw pushes the tongue backwards; our article on <a href="/blogs/can-jaw-surgery-help-with-sleep-apnea/">how the jaws affect the airway</a> explains this in more detail</li>
  <li><strong>Alcohol, sleeping tablets and some sedating antihistamines</strong>, which relax the throat further</li>
  <li><strong>Sleeping on your back</strong>, which lets the tongue fall backwards</li>
  <li><strong>Smoking</strong>, which inflames the lining of the nose and throat</li>
  <li><strong>Pregnancy, menopause and ageing</strong>, which change muscle tone and soft tissues</li>
</ul>
<p>Simple snoring, sometimes called primary snoring, means snoring without breathing pauses, oxygen drops or daytime symptoms. It can strain relationships and disturb a partner's sleep, but on its own it is not thought to carry the health risks linked with sleep apnea.</p>

<h2 id="what-makes-it-apnea">What turns snoring into sleep apnea?</h2>
<p>In obstructive sleep apnea, the airway does more than vibrate: it partly or completely collapses. Breathing becomes shallow (a hypopnea) or stops (an apnea) for ten seconds or longer, the oxygen level falls, and the brain briefly rouses you — usually without your knowing — to reopen the airway. This can happen dozens of times an hour, every night.</p>
<p>That is why the sound pattern often changes. Simple snoring tends to be steady and rhythmic. Snoring with sleep apnea is often loud and then suddenly silent while breathing stops, followed by a snort or gasp as it restarts. There is also a middle ground, sometimes called upper airway resistance, in which breathing effort rises and sleep is fragmented without full apneas; it can still cause daytime tiredness.</p>
<p>The relationship does not run only one way, either. Some people with significant sleep apnea snore only softly, and women in particular may notice fatigue, insomnia, morning headaches or low mood rather than loud snoring. A quiet bedroom is not proof of healthy breathing.</p>
<h3>Why the difference matters for your health</h3>
<p>Each breathing pause in sleep apnea triggers a fall in oxygen and a surge of stress hormones that raise heart rate and blood pressure. Over months and years, untreated moderate to severe sleep apnea is associated with high blood pressure, atrial fibrillation, heart failure, stroke, type 2 diabetes and low mood. The daytime sleepiness it causes also increases the risk of road and workplace accidents — a real concern for anyone facing a long daily commute or who drives for a living. Simple snoring has not been shown to carry these risks to the same degree. That is the practical reason for telling the two apart: one is mainly a nuisance, the other is a medical condition that benefits from treatment.</p>

<h2 id="signs-compared">Snoring vs sleep apnea: signs compared</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Simple snoring</th><th>Possible sleep apnea</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Sound</td><td>Steady and rhythmic</td><td>Loud and irregular; silences followed by snorts or gasps</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Breathing pauses</td><td>None noticed</td><td>Often seen by a partner or family member</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Waking at night</td><td>Occasional</td><td>Frequent; may wake choking or with a pounding heart</td></tr>
    <tr><td>On waking</td><td>Usually refreshed</td><td>Unrefreshed, with a dry mouth or morning headache</td></tr>
    <tr><td>During the day</td><td>Normal alertness</td><td>Sleepiness, poor concentration, irritability</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Night-time urination</td><td>Uncommon</td><td>Often two or more times a night</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Not affected by snoring itself</td><td>Linked with high or hard-to-control blood pressure</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pattern</td><td>Often only after alcohol, a cold or exhaustion</td><td>Most nights, whatever the circumstances</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>No single row decides it. A person can tick several boxes in the right-hand column and still have only mild sleep apnea, or tick few and have more than expected. The table is a guide to whether you need testing, not a substitute for it.</p>

