<h2 id="check-at-home">Clues you can check at home</h2>
<p>None of these can diagnose sleep apnea, but they help you and your doctor decide how soon to test:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ask your partner to watch.</strong> Does the snoring stop for several seconds and restart with a gasp? How often in, say, ten minutes?</li>
  <li><strong>Record yourself.</strong> A phone voice recorder or snoring app can capture the pattern, and silences followed by gasps are worth showing to a doctor. Apps cannot reliably measure oxygen or confirm apneas.</li>
  <li><strong>Score your sleepiness.</strong> The Epworth Sleepiness Scale asks how likely you are to doze in eight ordinary situations, such as reading, watching television or sitting in traffic. A high score suggests excessive sleepiness.</li>
  <li><strong>Try a STOP-BANG check.</strong> This eight-question screen combines snoring, tiredness, observed pauses, blood pressure, BMI, age, neck size and sex. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/sleep-apnea-symptoms-in-adults/">sleep apnea symptoms in adults</a> walks you through it.</li>
  <li><strong>Notice what changes it.</strong> If snoring appears only after alcohol or when your nose is blocked, those triggers are worth tackling first.</li>
</ul>
<p>The only way to know for sure is a sleep study — either an overnight study in a sleep laboratory or, for suitable adults, a home sleep apnea test — which records breathing and oxygen levels and, in the laboratory, sleep itself.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Do not wait if</strong> you have fallen asleep while driving or nearly had an accident because of sleepiness, someone has seen you stop breathing repeatedly, or you snore and have a heart rhythm problem, hard-to-control blood pressure or a past stroke. Avoid driving when drowsy and arrange an assessment promptly. For chest pain, sudden breathlessness or any emergency, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department.</p>
</div>
<p>Book a routine assessment if you snore loudly most nights and wake unrefreshed, if your snoring is disturbing your partner's sleep, if your nose is blocked most of the time, or if you have a receding chin or bite problem along with snoring. Children who snore on most nights, breathe through the mouth, pause in their sleep or have behaviour or concentration problems should see a paediatrician or ENT specialist, because the causes and treatment differ from adults.</p>
<p>A sleep physician confirms the diagnosis. A maxillofacial surgeon examines the structures that shape the airway — the nose, palate, tongue, jaws and bite — and how they relate to one another. At Gnathos Facial in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, we work alongside sleep physicians and ENT colleagues so that each part of the problem is addressed. Our <a href="/obstructive-sleep-apnea">sleep apnea treatment page</a> explains what the assessment involves.</p>

<h2 id="treatment-options">Treating snoring and sleep apnea: options and trade-offs</h2>
<h3>First, rule out sleep apnea</h3>
<p>This is the most important step. Procedures that make snoring quieter can reduce the noise without fixing blocked breathing, leaving sleep apnea untreated and harder to spot. If your history suggests sleep apnea, have a sleep study before treating the snoring itself.</p>
<h3>For simple snoring</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lifestyle changes</strong> — weight loss, avoiding alcohol in the evening, stopping smoking and sleeping on your side</li>
  <li><strong>Treating the nose</strong> — managing allergies and, where there is a structural blockage, septoplasty or turbinate reduction</li>
  <li><strong>A custom oral appliance</strong> made by a dentist to hold the lower jaw slightly forward during sleep</li>
  <li><strong>Palate or tonsil procedures</strong> in selected cases, once sleep apnea has been excluded</li>
</ul>
<h3>For sleep apnea</h3>
<p>CPAP remains first-line for moderate to severe sleep apnea. Oral appliances, positional therapy, weight management, nasal treatment and, for carefully selected adults, jaw surgery are alternatives or additions; our guide to <a href="/blogs/cpap-alternatives-for-sleep-apnea/">CPAP alternatives</a> compares them.</p>
<h3>Honest expectations</h3>
<p>Every option has trade-offs. Oral appliances can cause tooth or jaw discomfort and slow changes to the bite. Nasal surgery brings a few days of congestion and a week or two of recovery, and it improves nasal breathing more reliably than it silences snoring. Throat surgery can be sore for one to two weeks, and snoring sometimes returns over time. Jaw surgery is a major operation with weeks of recovery and risks such as numbness of the lip and chin. Results vary from person to person, and a follow-up review — and, for sleep apnea, a repeat sleep study — is part of good care.</p>
